    "synthesis": "Confirmed independently. The binding numbers reproduce exactly: `L_y(worst_9) = 38681/79380 = 0.487289`, margin to cap_9 = 0.006971, and worst < consec. All three k-values match the evidence package. I now have everything I need to render the four decisions.\n\n---\n\n**DECISION — LRC(14) ENDGAME ASSESSMENT**\n\n**(1) Is the GAP DIMENSION PENALTY proved or close? — NOT PROVED. The original framing is broken; a sharper, harder lemma replaces it.**\n\nStatus: STRONG COMPUTATIONAL CONFIRMATION, ZERO closed proof. The angle's working premise — \"the dimension penalty is large (margin ≥0.21) so a crude bound closes it\" — is FALSE and must be retired. Two independent adversarial verifications (verify_gap_dim_penalty_indep.py, verify_stratum_claim_opus.py) agree:\n\n- The ≥0.21 margin only applies to SPREAD d≥2 GAPs (Minkowski sums of two genuine APs). Those are the easy, non-binding cases.\n- The actual supremum over all non-full-AP k-sets is attained NOT by a spread GAP but by a **minimal single-end-defect near-AP** (excess=2, one double-step): the UNIQUE k=9 maximizer is E=[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,9] with L_y = 38681/79380 = 0.487289 (just reconfirmed). The margin to cap_9 is **0.00697** — three orders of magnitude tighter than the angle hoped.\n\nPrecise remaining gap (now split into two sub-lemmas, per the synthesis):\n- **3(a-i) spread GAPs:** large penalty (≥0.157 at k=8, ≥0.21 typical), provable via the orbit-collision identity O(A1+A2)(x) = O(A1)(x) ⊕ O(A2)(x). EASY. Not yet written but low-risk.\n- **3(a-ii) near-AP boundary:** the binding case. Must prove L_y(single-defect near-AP) < cap_k for the k=9 family at margin 0.00697. A crude bound CANNOT close this. Two open pieces: **(A)** prove the global sup over ALL non-full-AP k-sets (unbounded max(E)) is attained at the s=2 single-end-defect — currently exhaustive only to max(E)≤18; **(B)** prove the exact inequality 38681/79380 < cap_9 with a structural reason for the ~0.0056 drop from consec.\n\n**(2) Does the Freiman partition fully cover the configuration space (no third pocket)? — YES, coverage is complete, but via a DIFFERENT mechanism than the binary dichotomy.**\n\nThe intended binary split [small-doubling bounded GAP] | [large-doubling dissociated stranger] is the WRONG abstraction and partitions nothing: (i) every 8–10-element set sits in SOME proper 2-dim GAP, so \"GAP existence\" absorbs 100% of sets; (ii) the \"dissociated stranger\" type is nearly vacuous since 8–10 integers almost always admit a low-height relation. **There is no third pocket** — but the reason is that L_y is CONTINUOUSLY controlled by the doubling σ = |E+E|/k, and max-L_y is monotone non-increasing in σ (verified exhaustively in the binding k=9 low-spread regime, spread≤16, 12868 sets). The configuration space is covered by a one-parameter envelope, not a structural trichotomy. The \"gcd-stratum carries the danger\" sub-hypothesis (HYP-2640 region) is REFUTED: the cap-approach danger is an **archimedean integer-height** phenomenon (low-height relation defects like 2a−8, 7a−28), not a mod-27 / gcd-3 phenomenon. The apparent stratum effect was a position confound (verified flat across strata in the controlled test).\n\n**(3) What does the recursion in n reveal — is n=14 (C=3³) special, and does it give a recursive reduction? — n=14 IS genuinely the tightest, but NO recursive reduction exists.**\n\nVerified: n=14 is the UNIQUE non-trivial prime power in the range (C=27=3³), and on the binding |P|=5 row it has the uniquely thin non-degenerate margin (+0.054; next is n=18 at +0.100). The clean recursion p_all-missed(consec_k) = 1/((n/2)(k−1)) holds exactly for all even n=8..20. The 3³ divisor chain 1|3|9|27 (nested strata) is why n=14's margin is structurally thinnest — this is a real, verified DIAGNOSTIC correlation. BUT: no LRC(n)→LRC(n−2) reduction was established. The sector-peel raises meas_S at fixed k (wrong direction), and C(n)−C(n−2)=4 is additive not multiplicative, so the dimension-peel does not yield a clean inequality. The n-analysis explains WHY n=14 is hard; it does not help close it.\n\n**(4) Single most valuable next step.**\n\nProve sub-lemma **3(a-ii)(B)**: the exact inequality for the binding k=9 single-defect family, via an **analytic L_y ≤ φ(σ) envelope** where φ is derived from the orbit-collision/Minkowski-defect mechanism, with φ(2) < cap_9 and φ non-increasing. Concretely: derive a closed expression for the L_y *drop* caused by a single end-defect (the verified −0.0056-from-consec, −0.01446 in p_0) in terms of the one low-height integer relation it introduces (2a−(k+1)), then show this drop exceeds the consec-to-cap slack (consec_9 sits only 0.00138 below cap, so the defect must cost > that). This is the genuine crux and the only step where the margin is razor-thin. Pair it with closing sub-lemma (A) — bounding max(E) so the exhaustive search becomes a proof (a height bound H from the small-doubling/Freiman structure makes the single-defect family the certified global sup).\n\n**Honest distance to a complete proof.** LRC(14) is **NOT proved.** The proof program is now well-localized: steps (1)/(2)/3(a-i) are essentially done or low-risk; the entire difficulty is concentrated in one tight one-parameter lemma 3(a-ii) at k=9 with margin 0.00697, plus the unbounded-spread tail bound (the standing HYP-2610 large-doubling lemma). Every numerical claim across all four angles is exact-Fraction-verified with no counterexample, and the binding maximizer is reconfirmed here. But \"no counterexample in an exhaustive bounded search\" is not a proof — the closed argument for the tight k=9 inequality and the unbounded-max(E) certification do not yet exist. Distance: one hard analytic lemma (the φ(σ) envelope at the near-AP boundary) plus one routine tail bound away from complete.\n\nRelevant scripts (absolute):\n- C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\04-computation\\lrc14_empty_sector_distribution_kps.py (engine, reconfirmed here)\n- C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\04-computation\\verify_gap_dim_penalty_indep.py\n- C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\04-computation\\verify_stratum_claim_opus.py\n- C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\04-computation\\lrc_runner_adversarial_verify.py\n- C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\05-knowledge\\results\\lrc_runner_margin_adversarial.out"
  }
